lements (consortium standard) AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations. Yes.\n\nEvidence sentence: The proposal reframes mandated documentation as a scientific research object and creates new scholarly outputs from compliance, altering the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nScore 4.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nOpen standards consortium for AI documentation is somewhat unusual? Many standards initiatives exist. But open provenance-standards consortium specifically making documentation quality measurable research variable? Might be unusual interesting. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not sure if not expressed before and ingenious. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: It proposes a two-year multi-stakeholder working group to publish an open scientific reference standard with schema tooling, which is unusual for a lab compliance response.\n\nScore 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is about technical documentation standard per Recital 71, not directly logging mandate in Article 12? Recital 71 mentions technical documentation and logs. Article 12 is logging. Proposal focuses on documentation standards, not logging capabilities for events. It may be related but not directly solves logging mandate. It applies to broader compliance documentation.\n\nDoes it apply? The problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Creating standard for documentation does relate to Recital 71 purpose, but Article 12 is about automatic recording of events logs. Documentation standard may help compliance generally but not specifically logging mandate.\n\nApplies to stated problem? Maybe implied problem: improving compliance infrastructure. Might be applicability 3 = solves implied problem related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y which applies). It does not directly solve logging mandate. Could be 2.\n\nLet's think: The proposal is about technical documentation described in Recital 71, system characteristics, algorithms, data, validation processes. Article 12 requires logs. So it's partially related but not logging. The stated problem explicitly mentions how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal adapts R&D to compliance by making documentation a research variable. That's adaptation.\n\nProbably applicability 3: solves implied problem that is related: improving compliance readiness / documentation quality for high-risk AI systems, which is part of overall regulatory burden.\n\nEvidence: The proposal targets technical documentation mandated by Recital 71 rather than the automatic event logging required by Article 12, addressing a related compliance need.\n\nScore 3? Or 2? \"solves an implied problem that is related to the stated problem (do X to get an implied Y, which applies to the stated problem)\". Might be 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? No. It will contribute to compliance documentation but not solve logging. Might be partial contribution to broader compliance burden. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Or maybe 2 unreasonable? Could improve documentation quality which is part of compliance.\n\nI think effectiveness 3.\n\nEvidence: A shared documentation standard would improve traceability and post-market monitoring readiness, contributing to overall compliance even though it does not provide automatic logs.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nTwo-year working group convening researchers, regulatory experts, deployers, drafting open-access publications with schema tooling. That's significant resource commitment, coordination across stakeholders. For a single lab, that's expensive and difficult. Probably 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nEvidence: Coordinating a multi-stakeholder two-year standards effort with schema tooling requires substantial staff time, coordination costs, and external commitments beyond routine compliance.\n\nScore 2.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates\n\nOpen standards consortium is acceptable, even desirable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nEvidence: Publishing open-access documentation standards and collaborating with regulators aligns with scientific norms and regulatory expectations.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence then score.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
