e changes, reasonably feasible measures? Cross-project team of three engineers plus one data-governance lead over two sprints, validate against two projects, publish to portal. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Could be 3. Maybe 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Two sprints with 4 people is modest. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Standardizing internal spec is common. So 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring? Developing reusable architecture spec for compliance is fairly standard best practice. Not unusual. Could be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting. I think it's common. Might be 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. This is standard compliance practice. It introduces new elements? Reusable spec is maybe slightly paradigm stretching? Definition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nProposal is standardizing logging architecture - that's still treating compliance as logging practice. Could be slightly paradigm stretching because reusable spec introduces new element. Hmm. The description: \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". That fits. Might be 2. Paradigm preserving would be just do per-project logging. Reusable spec is a new process. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nLet's choose originality 1? Common mundane. Could be 2 interesting. The reference class: experienced evaluator would expect labs to give reusable spec? Probab