iance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This seems exactly that: framing compliance as research changes relationship. Could be 4.\n\nBut maybe 3 is paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). The difference between 4 and 3 subtle. 4 requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does introduce compressed schema and open standard. Might be breaking.\n\nI'll choose 3? Let's think: paradigm breaking would be e.g., stop doing biometric research altogether. This is still compliance logging practice but reframed as research. I'd say paradigm stretching: changes that relationship. Not breaking.\n\nChoose paradigm_relatedness = 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before (rare, unusual) AND ingenious, imaginative, or surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nDeveloping open compressed log schema for EU AI Act compliance and publishing as research. Is this common? Experienced evaluator would expect labs to do standard logging implementation, maybe vendor solution. Framing as open research publication is somewhat unusual but not extremely ingenious. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nMaybe 2 = interesting. I'd lean 3.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nTwo postdocs for six months benchmarking, release reference implementation. That's reasonably feasible measures, some changes. Cost of two postdocs is not trivial but feasible. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.