sting telemetry to regulation is common, mundane. So 1.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve problem of how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D? It identifies gaps, prevents duplicate development, freeing capacity. That contributes to meeting mandate efficiently. Does it solve? It doesn't actually implement logs, just maps. So partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. Or 4? If you could do it, would it solve main problem? Mapping alone doesn't provide logs, just identifies gaps. So not full solve. So 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 =...\n\nMapping telemetry clearly applies to stated problem of meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nFour-week audit with one-page inventory per group, compliance engineering produces gap matrix. That's easy low cost. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is standard audit, acceptable. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably just list. Instructions: Return rating... For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely as key-value pairs.\n\nWill output:\n\nid: P1650\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 1\n...\n\nMake sure evidence one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a standard l