adigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nShared middleware introduces new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces tool but compliance remains logging practice. Probably 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nShared logging middleware for all pipelines is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably output with fields.\n\nNeed echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProvide as list? Probably simple lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2915\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nImplementability evidence: The SDK can be bolted onto existing MLOps and experiment-tracking stack with central audit store, requiring moderate integration work across projects but using standard instrumentation patterns. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Centralized logging middleware is a common compliance practice that does not violate legal norms or business sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly auto-captures Article 12(2) event categories for high-risk systems, addressing the mandated logging requirement. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, it would provide automatic, timestamped traceability