odifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal introduces two-tier methodology, science-to-system transition criterion, SDK to swap instrumentation. It changes relationship between R&D and compliance? It weaves logging into research lifecycle, keeps exploratory iteration. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Not breaking? It doesn't change what lab researches. It still treats compliance as logging practice but with phased. I'd say 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship.\n\nLet's pick scores:\n\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 4? Hmm maybe 3 because it only contributes? The mandate requires automatic recording over lifetime. Phased still does full at deployment. Might be fully effective. I'll go 4.\n\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\noriginality 3\nparadigm_relatedness 3\n\nProvide evidence sentences.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first and then \"...\". Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll produce structured output with id P1457 and then entries.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1457\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses how the lab meets Article 12 logging while adapting R&D workflow.\napplicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, full Article 12(3) logging is guaranteed at deployment and lightweight logs protect early iteration, solving both compliance and velocity concerns.\neffectiveness:4\n\nacceptability_evidence: Phased compliance with documented gatekeeping aligns with regulatory intent and does not violate legal or scientific norms.\nacceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a shared SDK and a documented transition criterion requires moderate engineering effort but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplementability:3\n\noriginality_evidence: Two-tier logging with swappable instrumentation depth is unusual and shows imagination beyond standard one-size logging.\noriginality:3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It stretches the paradigm by weaving compliance instrumentation into the science lifecycle rather than treating logging as a post-hoc administrative add-on.\nparadigm_relatedness:3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
