        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2220\",\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"The proposal builds a version-controlled archive of lifetime inference logs with semantic tagging, directly providing the automatic event recording required by Article 12 for high-risk systems.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"If implemented, the longitudinal log archive would satisfy traceability, post-market monitoring and risk identification logging mandates without regard to cost.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 4,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Creating an open-access, ontology-tagged longitudinal model-drift archive is unusual for compliance logging and shows imagination beyond standard record-keeping.\",\n      \"originality\": 3,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It transforms mandatory logging into a scientific data product and partnership engine, changing how R&D and compliance obligations relate rather than just adding tools.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 3,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Public release of de-identified inference logs from high-risk AI systems raises privacy and confidentiality concerns that make the open-data commitment socially and legally sensitive.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 2,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Extending middleware with a semantic tagging ontology, maintaining a version-controlled lifetime archive, and ensuring safe annual de-identification requires substantial engineering and legal work.\",\n      \"implementability\": 2\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2220: Longitudinal Model-Drift Archive as Open Data. Structured version-controlled archive of lifetime inference logs... extend Lab Director's middleware logging layer with semantic tagging ontology... release de-identified aggregate datasets under open-access licence annually. Positions lab as empirical reference point...\n\nRate subscales.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is about building archive of lifetime inference logs with semantic tagging and releasing open data. Does it apply? It addresses logging mandate. Yes solves identified problem directly related: meeting logging mandate via automatic recording of events over lifetime. Also extends. So applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Evidence sentence.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nIt will provide logs, traceability. Will it solve stated problem? It meets logging mandate, plus extra. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably 4? It would solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. Might be more than needed. Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Yes.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nArchive of lifetime inference logs as open data with semantic tagging ontology. Is that rare? Unusual interesting shows some imagination? Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not necessarily ingenious surprising? Could be 3. Maybe 4? Not expressed before AND ingenious? Probably not paradigm-breaking originality? Hmm. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal weaves logging into science and creates open data offering, changes relationship. It turns compliance into scientific impact. That's paradigm stretching? Possibly paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners...). Could be 3. Might be 4? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does change relationship, introduces archive as open data. Could be paradigm breaking? Maybe not break paradigm entirely, just stretch. I'd give 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nReleasing de-identified aggregate datasets of high-risk AI logs... Is that acceptable under EU AI Act? Possibly concerns about privacy, data protection, Article 12 logging includes input data for which search led to match etc. Releasing open access may violate GDPR, confidentiality. Might offend sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable? Could be 2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but is not totally unacceptable. Or 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. De-identification may still risk. I'd argue releasing logs from high-risk systems even de-identified may be legally risky, politically sensitive. So acceptability maybe 2? Common strategies violate no norms? No. It's somewhat uncommon and potentially offends sensibilities about data privacy. Might be 2.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nExtend middleware logging layer with semantic tagging ontology, version-controlled archive, release annually. That's significant change, expensive or difficult. Not totally impossible. So 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Hmm requires engineering, ontology design, de-identification pipeline, legal review. Significant change. I'd say 2.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating then score.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (an integer level). Might be lines.\n\nWe'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal builds a version-controlled archive of lifetime inference logs with semantic tagging, directly providing the automatic event recording required by Article 12 for high-risk systems.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the longitudinal log archive would satisfy traceability, post-market monitoring and risk identification logging mandates without regard to cost.\neffectiveness: 4\n\noriginality_evidence: Creating an open-access, ontology-tagged longitudinal model-drift archive is unusual for compliance logging and shows imagination beyond standard record-keeping.\noriginality: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It transforms mandatory logging into a scientific data product and partnership engine, changing how R&D and compliance obligations relate rather than just adding tools.\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Public release of de-identified inference logs from high-risk AI systems raises privacy and confidentiality concerns that make the open-data commitment socially and legally sensitive.\nacceptability: 2\n\nimplementability_evidence: Extending middleware with a semantic tagging ontology, maintaining a version-controlled lifetime archive, and ensuring safe annual de-identification requires substantial engineering and legal work.\nimplementability: 2\n\nReturn.\n\n",
