asy? Requires instrumentation of log emitters. Might be some changes. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Need code coverage on inference paths, spinning up ephemeral test. Might be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill this solve logging mandate? It ensures logs are produced with correct structure before deployment, but does it guarantee logging over lifetime? It checks log-emitter code coverage and events land in log store. That addresses completeness and schema validity, which helps meet Article 12. But does it guarantee recording of events relevant for risk situations etc? Partially. Might contribute but not fully solve traceability over lifetime. Could be partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, partial). Could be 4 if it solves main problem without regard workability: ensures logs exist. Hmm.\n\nThe mandate is automatic recording of events over lifetime with specific content. Gates ensure log emitter present before promotion. That seems directly effective at ensuring compliance for new deployments. Might solve stated problem? Stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This addresses meeting mandate by preventing non-compliant models from deploying. That's pretty effective.\n\nMaybe 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). I'd argue it ensures compliance compile-time, so yes.\n\nLet's pick effectiveness 3? Let's think: It checks log-emitter code coverage on modified inference paths and confirms events land with correct structure. Does not guarantee logs are complete for all required event types per Article 12(2)(a)-(c) or specific data for point 1(a) systems. So partial.