ty (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Repurposing internal tools for compliance is common. Acceptable. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Two-person pod one quarter mapping fields onto existing schema, adding only genuine gaps. Reasonably feasible, low cost? Maybe some changes. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. The description says avoids duplicating infrastructure and keeps costs within envelope. One quarter two engineers is moderate. Might be easy to implement at low cost? Hmm \"some changes\" maybe 3. Anchored: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Mapping mandated fields onto existing schema and adding gaps sounds like some changes. Probably 3.\n\nBut could be 4? It says extend internal platform already recording hyperparameters... adding only genuine gaps. That sounds non-radical. I think implementability 3 is safe.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nRepurposing experiment tracking for compliance is slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tool