ich idea preserves or modifies paradigm...\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces shared runtime layer, standard schema. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging. That's slightly paradigm stretching = 2.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Yes, automatically capture logs meeting Article 12 thresholds, standardize schema. Should solve. So 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. So 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... Provide lines.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2241\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProvide exactly.\n\n",
