ionship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal introduces gate before prototype, weaving logging into design. It changes relationship? Logging is woven into R&D process, not just after. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Could be 2: introduces new elements (review gate, spec) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Maybe 3? \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science)\". It says logging architecture review before prototype. That is weaving logging into science. Might be paradigm stretching =3.\n\nLet's choose 2 or 3. The description for 3: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). This seems like logging woven into science. Could be 3. Slightly paradigm stretching is introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Hmm difference subtle.\n\nI think it's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (gate, document) but still treats compliance as logging practice. I'd give 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nLog-by-design gate is fairly common in safety engineering. Might be common, mundane. Probably 1 or 2. \"common, mundane, boring\" -> 1. Could be 2 interesting. I'd say 1? Many labs have design reviews. Might be common. I'd rate 2 = interesting? Let's pick 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nProbably originality low. Let's choose 1 = common mundane boring. The idea of design gate for compliance is standard.\n\nLet's pick 2? Let's think: reference class set of responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many would propose logging architecture review. Might be common. So originality 1.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill identifying logging gaps at whiteboard prevent costly retrofits and keep deployable? Yes seems will solve. Reasonable and will solve. 4.\n\nSo final ratings:\nacceptability evidence sentence + score 4\nimplementability evidence + 3\nparadigm_relatedness evidence + 2 (or 3)\noriginality +1\napplicability +4\neffectiveness +4\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\". Probably JSON.\n\nProvide as maybe list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1403\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
