d problem directly? Problem is how to meet logging mandate. Study doesn't meet mandate, just models costs. So not direct.\n\nMaybe applicability = 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: The study models storage costs rather than implementing the required automatic event logs.\n\nNow effectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nWill it solve stated problem? It will contribute to solution? It gives numbers for negotiating tiered retention contracts, but does not implement logging. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (partial). Could be 3. Or 2 unreasonable/unlikely to solve problem. It doesn't actually produce logs. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes.\n\nEvidence: It provides cost forecasts that could inform retention choices but does not itself create the mandated logging capabilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nSix-month project with two grad researchers instrument three pilots collect data over eight weeks. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Implementability 3? Easy to implement at low cost or non-radical =4. Assigning two grads for six months is some changes. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires limited internal staff and pilot instrumentation that is feasible within an R&D lab.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Study costs is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Cost modeling for compliance is a common, non-controversial business practice.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nOptions:\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is a study about cost of logging. Introduces new elements? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2. Or 1 paradigm preserving? It's standard practice to assess costs before implementation. Probably paradigm preserving? Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is internal research project to model storage costs... That's typical cost analysis. Probably paradigm preserving =1. Standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. It doesn't change relationship. I'd say 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (middleware, compression study) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Maybe 2.\n\nLet's choose 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a cost-analysis middleware and pilot metrics but keeps compliance framed as conventional logging expense management.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious.\nCommon, mundane boring =1. This is common cost study. So originality 1.\n\nEvidence: Cost modeling of log retention is a common, mundane operational analysis in labs facing regulatory storage mandates.\n\nNow format: need echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nReturn rating.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id. Could be plain text.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2935\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure each evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
