ents: automatic recording events, period of use, reference database, input data match, verifier identification. Proposal covers only verifier identification. So contributes but not full solution.\n\nMight argue it solves the stated problem for that sub-requirement. The problem is overall logging mandate. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nAdds single authenticated sign-off screen to existing results-review interface, writing structured log line. That's some changes but seems reasonably feasible. Probably 3? Could be 4 = easy at low cost non-radical. Adding auth sign-off screen is modest. Might be 3. Let's pick 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4? \"minimising friction\" suggests easy. Hmm anchored: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes => 4. Adds single screen to existing tooling. That sounds non-radical. I'd say 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nIntegrating identity capture for compliance is common, no violation. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal integrates identity capture into human-in-loop review tooling, turning compliance into two-second click within existing workflow. Is that slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (authenticated sign-off screen, structured log) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nBuilding verifier attribution into deployment pipeline is fairly common compliance practice. Might be 1 = common mundane boring. Or 2 = interesting? Auto-recording via sign-off screen is standard. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nProvide output echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nFormat maybe:\nid: P2788\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's produce.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nEnsure order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nReturn rating.\n\n",
