troduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So level 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nRing-fencing capacity for compliance is common project management. Common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDedicated capacity ensures logging features ship on schedule. That would help meet mandate. It will contribute to solution but does it solve? Meeting logging mandate requires technical implementation of logs, not just capacity. Capacity helps but doesn't guarantee correct logs. So contributes, partial. Reasonable and will contribute. Level 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt directly addresses how to meet logging mandate via resource allocation. So solves identified problem directly related. Level 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nAllocate 15% senior engineering headcount is significant change, expensive. Senior engineers scarce. That's significant change, expensive or difficult. Not totally impossible. Level 2? Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Hmm 15% of senior headcount is substantial. I'd say significant change, expensive. Level 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nDedicating engineers to compliance is common strategy, does not violate norms. Level 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions say return rating... give ... Provide in order.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2880\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's produce clear lines.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces a dedicated capacity line and process change but still treats compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship. => level 2\n\nOriginality evidence: Ring-fencing engineering headcount for compliance is a common resource-allocation tactic with no ingenious or surprising element. => 1\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Dedicated capacity makes it more likely logging features ship on schedule, which contributes to meeting Article 12 but does not by itself guarantee correct log content or traceability. => 3\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses how the lab will resource and deliver the Article 12 logging mandate within its R&D pipeline. => 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Reallocating fifteen percent of senior engineering headcount represents a significant, expensive organizational change that will be difficult to sustain in a research-focused lab. => 2\n\nAcceptability evidence: Carving out compliance capacity is a conventional, nor