m compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nA feasibility study is standard due diligence. It introduces new element? Probably paradigm preserving. Might be slightly paradigm stretching because it introduces spike? I'd say paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. Score 1.\n\nOriginality: degree idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\nCommon, mundane, boring =1. Feasibility spike before build is common. Score 1.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem is must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\nThe proposal is a study to answer viability before commit budget. Does it solve problem? It helps decide but does not actually meet logging mandate. Effectiveness definition: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem) =4. Contribute to solution =3. Unreasonable =2. Solves unrelated =1.\n\nA feasibility study does not meet logging mandate; it only informs decision. It contributes to solution but is only partial solution. Probably effectiveness 3? Or 2? The stated problem that every proposal below addresses: an AI R&D lab... must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\n\nThe proposal is to do a feasibility study. That's exploring whether idea might someday work, which the problem says approval is NOT for funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So effectiveness is low? It does not solve logging mandate, it delays. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Might be 3 contributes.\n\nHmm instrument: effectiveness is degree to which idea will solve problem, without regard for workability. If you could do it, would it solve main problem? The main problem is meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. A spike does not meet mandate. It just assesses viability. So it does not solve. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but it is only partial solution). I'd say contributes to decision making. Probably 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nThe proposal is about logging viability for biometric systems, directly related to Article 12 mandate. So applicability 4? It solves identified problem directly related? The identified problem is how to meet logging mandate. Study does apply. Might be 4.\n\nBut note applicability vs effectiveness distinction. Could be applicability 4 because it clearly applies to stated problem (deciding how to respond). Even if not solve fully, it applies.\n\nLet's assign:\nimplementability_evidence: A four-week spike using existing staff to model volumes and costs is a low-cost, non-radical analysis that can be launched immediately.\nimplementability:4\n\nacceptability_evidence: Conducting an internal technical feasibility study is a common, norm-compliant business practice that offends no legal or social sensibilities.\nacceptability:4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal treats compliance as standard logging engineering to be validated before building, leaving the lab’s R&D and compliance relationship unchanged.\nparadigm_relatedness:1\n\noriginality_evidence: Pre-build