ceability is mandated by Article 12 and widely regarded as standard compliance practice.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is standardized logging module embedded at architecture level. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It says non-bypassable logging module embedded at architecture level before leaves lab. Might be more than slightly? It still treats compliance as logging practice. Likely 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a logging SDK and architectural constraint without redefining how R&D is organized or what is researched.\n\nScore 2.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nMandatory logging layer is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Embedding default logging SDKs for regulatory traceability is a common, mundane compliance measure in AI labs.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve? It captures events relevant to risk identification, substantial modifications, operational monitoring. Captures start/end timestamps, input data references, modification triggers auto-recorded. Article 12 requires logging of events relevant to risk etc., for high-risk systems point 1(a) Annex III requires period of use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Proposal mentions start/end timestamps, input data references, modification triggers. Might not fully cover all required items like identification of natural persons involved in verification. But overall will solve? Probably contributes but partial. It says protects from non-compliance penalties and ensures no system ships without traceability. Might be reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability -> 4? The problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Mandatory logging layer seems to directly solve. However does it address point 1(a) specifics? It mentions input data references but not reference database against which input checked, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Hmm.\n\nMight be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Auto-recording timestamps and modification triggers addresses core traceability needs but does not explicitly guarantee capture of all Annex III logging items such as verifier identities.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = ...\n\nClearly applies. 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal directly implements automatic event logging for high-risk pipelines as required by Article 12.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nFormat? Need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably lines.\n\nLet's output JSON? Probably just list.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid P2808\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order listed: implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, ef