d cost centre directly funds logging storage, retention and verification required by Article 12.\n\nScore 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Will ring-fencing budget solve logging mandate? It enables but doesn't itself implement logs. It contributes. Might be partial solution. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). So 3.\n\nEvidence: Visible funding alone does not build logging infrastructure but makes conformance work possible and prevents headcount erosion.\n\nScore 3.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nRing-fencing budget is standard financial governance, not changing relationship. It's paradigm preserving? Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introducing cost centre is new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance. Budget line is common strategy. I think paradigm_relatedness =1? Standard compliance practice. The instrument adapted to this problem. Might be 2 because introduces separate cost centre.\n\nLet's choose 2: introduces new element (standing cost centre) but compliance remains logging practice